<div class='content boxed'>

<h1 >SOAPBOXES</h1>
<h3>Welp</h3>
<p>I did my BArch as a mature student so I have some feelings<sup>TM</sup> from seeing the dire straits of my coursemates. And also from the looks of the industry.</p>
</ul>
</div><div class='content boxed' id="sleep">
<h3>GO THE FUCK TO SLEEP</h3> (I'm not kidding)
<p>I'm so serious about this.</p>
<p>Why do I care? Because the more we encourage a culture of skipping out on sleep the harder it is for anyone to look after their health. We need to all be on board for our own and each others' sakes.</p>
<p>It's optimistic to hope to gain much from an all-nighter... 2-5% on that one grade? The grade that affects 5% of the year's marks? You'll take twice as long to do twice as many mistakes. Do yourself a favour - take the L, submit what you have and use the time you'd be working into the night the next day to yourself out for the next piece of work.</p>
<h3>Health and relationship implications</h3>So what if i don't?
<p>This is a great time to find <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590142725000345">all</a> <a href="https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/dreaming-in-the-digital-age/201412/why-sleep-deprivation-is-torture">the</a> <a href="https://absnet.org.uk/2019/07/the-effect-of-a-good-nights-sleep/">ways</a> <a href="https://architizer.com/blog/inspiration/industry/change-my-mind-all-nighters/">that</a> <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381256484_Blueprints_of_Rest_Examining_the_Relation_between_Psychological_Well-being_and_Sleep_Quality_of_Architecture_Students">sleep</a> <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1389945707003036">deprivation</a> <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/16/17/3094">fucks</a> <a href="https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/humanrights/2025/04/03/exhausted-by-design-addressing-sleep-deprivation-as-a-human-rights-issue/">us</a> <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5449130/">up</a>. If you fuck up your sleep "just" during your studies it will come round to haunt you in ways you will regret.</p>
<h3>The Peer Pressure argument</h3>"yeah but everyone else is doing it"
<p>Yes, it's true that children like to brag or wail about their all-nighters. It then makes you feel like you can't compete, that you're one step behind.</p>
<p>So you have two choices: genuinely work hard in a way that will set yourself up for future success, or make your work culture worse and contribute to the peer pressure that makes your coursemates feel like they are also not allowed to sleep. I'd prefer not to be part of the group normalising it and invest in myself.</p>
<p>The trope of architects having shit work lives that fuck up their health and families needs to die. We can do this together, it starts in school, I believe in us.</p>
<h3>The myth of "being Realistic"</h3>"tHaT's nIcE iN tHeOry bUt iN rEaLiTy"
<p>In reality I did a care job alongside my BArch so I was pretty serious about managing the entirety of my coursework as a 9-5 while maintaining good grades. I did very few late nights the whole course, and by late nights I mean working til latest 11pm-1am instead of 6pm. I was doing this before I even got diagnosed with AuDHD. So I am coming from a very genuine place when I say that this is a realistic way of working for the vast majority of people.</p>
<h3>Adult workplace expectations</h3>"Yeah but my friends/ tutors/ parents expect it of me"
<p>The people who expect it of you don't care about your health. Or, at best, they believe your health is worth sacrificing to the altar of grade boundaries. Maybe that's what they know and and therefore think everyone should suffer the same as they did.</p>
<p>You will likely encounter many people in your career with all sorts of expectations that will hurt you and any other responsibilities you have. Do you believe that it'll be easier to learn to stand up for yourself when the stakes get even higher than grade boundaries? Practice advocating for yourself now so your future manager doesn't get comfortable fucking you over. We've got enough of that going on.</p>
<h3>It's not every time</h3>"Yeah but I put it off and now I'm in a panic"
<p>Look, I get it, sometimes things happen and stuff slips away. But.</p>
<p>The world won't end from one mark that's a bit lower than you'd like. If you're genuinely struggling to time manage on a regular basis, use this to figure out something that will actually work for you long-term. It's an investment. But if you keep crunching, all you're doing is developing a dependency on that and that dependency will hurt you.</p>
If literally one student decides to reduce their all-nighters that will make this website entirely worth the work I've put into this entire project.
</div><div class='content boxed' id="union">
<h3>Join a union</h3>
<p>Did you know there's a <a href="https://www.sawtheunion.org/">UK union of architectural workers</a>?</p>
<p><i>boy</i> am I relieved it does exist. Our industry is very used to having a very individualistic culture. It's actually a little embarassing imo. You can be a student member, employed or unemployed; the only thing that disqualifies you is if you have a role involved in employing someone else.</p>
</div>

